title: Controlled Codex worker via app-server wrapper
---
Build a c2c-managed Codex worker mode using Codex app-server/SDK instead of plain terminal polling. The immediate vanilla-Codex fix should remain c2c wait-inbox / poll-inbox --wait; this ticket tracks the richer implementation.

Problem:
- Vanilla Codex can send/poll with c2c CLI, but it is not continuously controllable unless the user manually polls or c2c wraps Codex.
- Recent Codex exposes app-server JSON-RPC over stdio/WebSocket/Unix socket and generated schemas with thread/start, thread/resume, turn/start, turn/steer, streamed notifications, and thread state flags.
- c2c could use that as a wrapper/control plane to keep a Codex worker reachable via c2c.

Sketch:
- Add a c2c managed Codex app-server worker mode, separate from simple CLI wait-inbox.
- Start or connect to codex app-server.
- Subscribe to the worker c2c inbox.
- On inbound c2c messages, call turn/start for idle threads or turn/steer when a steerable turn is active.
- Watch turn/started, agentMessageDelta, turn/completed, approval/user-input state, and activeTurnNotSteerable errors.
- Relay final/interim responses back via c2c where appropriate.
- Persist enough thread/session metadata for resume/restart.

Acceptance criteria:
- A c2c-managed Codex worker can receive a c2c DM and run a Codex turn without manual polling.
- The wrapper handles idle, running/steerable, and non-steerable active-turn states predictably.
- The worker reports status/errors through c2c and is restart/resume friendly.
- Docs explain when to use simple wait-inbox vs managed app-server worker mode.

Research notes: /tmp/c2c-codex-handoff.md and /tmp/c2c-codex-friction-points.md.